**Method 1: Using ODS PDF**

You can use the Output Delivery System (ODS) PDF statement to generate a PDF document with a journal format. Here's an example:
```sas
ods pdf file="journal.pdf";
ods layout gridded rows=2 columns=2;
ods statement name="Authors";
ods statement name="Title";
ods statement name="Abstract";
ods statement name="Introduction";
ods statement name="Results";
ods statement name="Discussion";
ods statement name="Conclusion";
ods statement name="References";
ods layout;
ods pdf close;
```
This code will generate a PDF document with a journal format, with each section (Authors, Title, etc.) on a separate page.

**Method 2: Using RTFOUT Procedure**

The RTFOUT procedure in SAS allows you to generate a Rich Text Format (RTF) file, which can be converted to a PDF document. Here's an example:
```sas
proc rtfout file="journal.rtf" style="journal";
  section "Authors";
  section "Title";
  section "Abstract";
  ...;
  section "References";
proc rtfout;
```
This code will generate an RTF file, which can then be converted to a PDF document using a tool like Ghostscript or Adobe Acrobat.

**Method 3: Using Report Procedure**

The Report procedure in SAS allows you to generate a report with a custom format. Here's an example:
```sas
ods report file="journal.pdf";
  compute section titled "Authors" {style<header>};
    ...;
  compute section titled "Title" {style<header>};
    ...;
  ...;
compute section titled "References" {style<header>};
    ...;
ods report;
```
This code will generate a PDF document with a custom report format, which can be tailored to match a journal format.

**Additional Tips**

* To customize the layout and formatting of the PDF document, you can use various ODS statements, such as `ods layout`, `ods column`, `ods row`, and `ods style`.
* To include tables, figures, and equations in your PDF document, you can use procedures like `proc print`, `proc sgplot`, and `proc equation`.
